Implement a generic `MinHeap<T>` class in TypeScript in file `heap.ts`.

## Required API

```ts
class MinHeap<T> {
  constructor(comparator: (a: T, b: T) => number);
  push(item: T): void;      // O(log n)
  pop(): T | undefined;     // O(log n) — removes and returns min
  peek(): T | undefined;    // O(1) — returns min without removing
  get size(): number;       // O(1)
  toSortedArray(): T[];     // returns elements in sorted order (non-destructive)
}
```

## Required test cases (must pass with `ts-node heap.ts`)

```ts
// Test 1: numbers
const h = new MinHeap<number>((a, b) => a - b);
[5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2].forEach(n => h.push(n));
console.assert(h.peek() === 1, "peek should be 1");
console.assert(h.pop() === 1, "first pop should be 1");
console.assert(h.pop() === 2, "second pop should be 2");
console.assert(h.size === 4, "size should be 4 after 2 pops");

// Test 2: objects by priority field
type Task = { name: string; priority: number };
const taskHeap = new MinHeap<Task>((a, b) => a.priority - b.priority);
taskHeap.push({ name: "low", priority: 10 });
taskHeap.push({ name: "high", priority: 1 });
taskHeap.push({ name: "mid", priority: 5 });
console.assert(taskHeap.pop()?.name === "high", "should pop highest priority task");

// Test 3: toSortedArray
const h2 = new MinHeap<number>((a, b) => a - b);
[4, 2, 7, 1].forEach(n => h2.push(n));
const sorted = h2.toSortedArray();
console.assert(JSON.stringify(sorted) === '[1,2,4,7]', "toSortedArray failed");
console.assert(h2.size === 4, "toSortedArray should be non-destructive");
```

## Constraints
- Pure TypeScript, no external libraries
- Compiles with `tsc --strict heap.ts`
- Internal storage must be an array (array-backed binary heap)
- Max 100 lines

## Acceptance criteria
- All 3 test groups pass when run with `ts-node heap.ts`
- No import of external libraries

Implement a `Trie` struct in Rust in a file `trie.rs`.

## Required public API

```rust
pub struct Trie { /* your fields */ }

impl Trie {
    pub fn new() -> Self;
    pub fn insert(&mut self, word: &str);
    pub fn search(&self, word: &str) -> bool;
    pub fn starts_with(&self, prefix: &str) -> bool;
}
```

## Required test cases (must pass with `cargo test` or `rustc`)

```rust
let mut t = Trie::new();
t.insert("apple");
t.insert("app");
assert!(t.search("apple"));          // true
assert!(t.search("app"));            // true
assert!(!t.search("ap"));            // false
assert!(t.starts_with("app"));       // true
assert!(t.starts_with("appl"));      // true
assert!(!t.starts_with("banana"));   // false
t.insert("banana");
assert!(t.search("banana"));         // true
assert!(!t.search("ban"));           // false
assert!(t.starts_with("ban"));       // true
```

## Constraints
- No external crates (std only)
- `trie.rs` must be a standalone file compilable with `rustc trie.rs --test`
- Include a `#[cfg(test)]` module with all 10 assertions above as individual `#[test]` functions

## Acceptance criteria
- `rustc trie.rs --test && ./trie` → all tests pass with exit code 0
- All 10 assert conditions from above are present as separate test functions
- No use of HashMap for the children (must use array or Vec<Option<Box<TrieNode>>>)
